Conformance
Id	Clause
conformance.1	This module specification describes the module that reads Claude Code's recording of the raw API bodies [CLAUDE-CODE] as the input values of the living-memory specification, each kept unit once. It is a module specification of the living-memory specification, and restates nothing that specification states; the schema of the kept units is that of the messages module.
conformance.2	The key words "MUST", "MUST NOT", "REQUIRED", "SHALL", "SHALL NOT", "SHOULD", "SHOULD NOT", "RECOMMENDED", "NOT RECOMMENDED", "MAY", and "OPTIONAL" in this specification are to be interpreted as described in BCP 14 [BCP14] when, and only when, they appear in all capitals, as shown here.
conformance.3	The Rules are normative. A rule's Id names what it governs: recording, what the module reads; values, the input values it gives. This specification has no optional features.
Rules
Id	Rule
recording.1	With OTEL_LOG_RAW_API_BODIES set to file:<dir>, each successful response appends one line to <dir>/index.jsonl: timestamp, session_id, query_source, model, request_id, message_id, message_uuid, request_file and response_file; the request body is the JSON-serialized Messages API request parameters, and the response body the JSON-serialized Messages API response [CLAUDE-CODE]. The module MUST read a folder that holds index.jsonl, each of its lines a JSON value, the first value 1 [JSONL]; the session transcript is not read, its format being internal to Claude Code [CLAUDE-CODE].
recording.2	Each request carries the entire conversation history [CLAUDE-CODE], so the same unit comes again in every later request of its conversation; Claude's extended thinking is always redacted from the bodies [CLAUDE-CODE].
recording.3	A request_file or response_file that is not an absolute path MUST be read in the recording's folder.
recording.4	An index line may name no request file, and may be written before the files it names. The module MUST stop at a line whose files are not yet written, and read it at the next conversion; a line that names no request file MUST give no unit, its response being re-sent in the conversation's next request [CLAUDE-CODE].
values.1	A unit is a system block, or the system prompt where it is a string, a tool, a message, or a response's content, which is the message that follows [ANTHROPIC-API]. The module MUST give each unit once, in the order its conversation first gives it: a request's system, tools and messages as the request holds them, then its response's content.
values.2	A unit is keyed by its conversation, the index line's session_id and query_source, its JSON Pointer in the request [RFC6901], the message that follows a response taking the pointer that message has, and its version, counted from 0 [CHEN1976]. Each value MUST be an object of session_id, query_source, request holding pointer, version, from, the number of messages in the request that first gives the unit, the request that follows for a response's content, and line, the index line that first gives it [WICKHAM2014], and MUST hold the unit under the member that holds it: system, tools, messages or content.
values.3	Two instances are equal when of the same type and value [JSON-SCHEMA-07]. A unit equal to the one its conversation's last request or response gave at its pointer MUST NOT be given again; a message re-sent after a response MUST be compared by its role, assistant, and its content; a unit unequal to it, or at a pointer it did not give, MUST be given as the next version at its address.
values.4	The module MUST read only the index lines after the last line the output holds, comparing the first of each conversation with that conversation's last request and response the output holds.
